    "created_at": "2021-03-18T03:03:56Z",
    "updated_at": "2021-03-18T03:03:56Z",
    "body": "ok, I like what you said elsewhere about splitting by kind: hyperlink VS (fetch-able) external resource, and then having types opt-in. The we can have a very clear delineation between what's a resource link and what's a hyperlink.  \r\n\r\nThe reason I suggested opt-out was because I figured it was more future proof and less of a footgun: If we end up adding a new attribute that affects an external resource type, then it automatically gets the benefits of the new attribute. \r\n\r\nIn any case, both models work well particularly at the spec level, as ultimately, it's the responsibility those editing the HTML spec that everything is wired up properly. \r\n\r\nI'm now envisioning prose like:\r\n\r\n\"The `<dfn for=\"link/rel\" attr-value>`manifest`</dfn>` link relationship is a [=external resource=] whose fetching behavior is governed by [=crossorigin=], [=integrity=], and [=referrerpolicy=] attributes. The `x`, `y`, `z`, attributes have no effect.\" Or something like that. Better still, it might be good to have a table listing each type, where one can look up what attributes have an effect and which don't. \r\n\r\nSomething like:\r\n\r\n<table>\r\n   <summary>External resource links</summary>\r\n   <tr><th>Name</th><th>crossorigin</th><th>integrity</th><th>referrerpolicy</th><th>media</th></tr>\r\n   <tr><td>manifest</td><td>✅</td><td>✅</td><td>✅</td><td>❌</td>\r\n   <tr><td>icon</td><td>✅</td><td>✅</td><td>✅</td><td>✅</td>\r\n   <tr><td>style</td><td>✅</td><td>✅</td><td>✅</td><td>✅</td>\r\n   <tr><td>Whatever</td><td>❌</td><td>❌</td><td>✅</td><td>❌</td>\r\n</table>\r\n\r\nThen if we add a new attribute, we are forced to think about how it affects each type... just a thought.   \r\n\r\n\r\n",
    "author_association": "MEMBER",
    "pin": null,
