    "body": "Spent some time looking at `rel=\"icon\"` to see if we could more fully specify this (at least, by inspecting source -- rigorously testing this would be a bit more work). It's...a bit of a wild west out there.\r\n\r\nChromium:\r\n* updates when navigation completes, a new applicable `<link>` is found or modified, or the media query state changes\r\n* respects `media`\r\n* does not seem to respect `integrity`, `nonce`, `crossorigin`, `referrerpolicy`, `fetchpriority`\r\n* stable-sorts by correspondence with desired size\r\n\r\nWebKit:\r\n* updates only when navigation completes\r\n* does not seem to respect `media`, `integrity`, `nonce`, `crossorigin`, `referrerpolicy`, `fetchpriority`\r\n* seems to rejects non-HTTP URLs (which would seem to prevent using, e.g., data: URLs)\r\n* unstable-sorts by total size (and ignores the second dimension but just parses the whole size as an integer, with a comment saying this is for compat)\r\n* asks WebKit's embedder (I assume in closed-source Safari code) to make a decision, which probably doesn't negate the above but passes the full attribute list as an `NSDictionary` in recent macOS/iOS releases, so can't be sure\r\n\r\nI was not able to find Gecko's counterpart code in searchfox (I found something called \"places\" which seems to be used for history and bookmarks, but not the code that actually operates on the HTMLLinkElement aside from exposing the \"sizes\" getter etc).\r\n\r\nMy instinct would be that these should respect dynamic changes, `media`, and the various attributes that affect fetch behavior (which is, in total, much closer to Chromium's current behavior). But WebKit also has comments suggesting that they're uncertain about the UI impact of the icon potentially changing, and it might be kinda weird to respect media at all if you're not going to respect changes to it (e.g., the icon gets frozen as a dark-mode icon even after you change to light mode), which makes it a little stranger to specify a processing model that makes respecting those optional but parsing processing media queries mandatory.",
